the building. A careful synthesis may therefore leave an important historical question open. Its value lies in making that uncertainty understandable without presenting an interval estimate as a settled event date.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "Its value would lie in making that uncertainty understandable", "revisedQuote": "Its value lies in making that uncertainty understandable", "problem": "The revision makes the proposed study's conditional value categorical; retain the qualification."}, {"originalQuote": "It cannot resolve the uncertain association between the fragment and the building [S1].", "revisedQuote": "The proposed study concerns the communication of existing evidence, not a new dating analysis [S1].", "problem": "Moving the citation attributes the study's scope to S1, which describes the archaeological evidence rather than the proposed communication study."}]}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "title": "Controlled source packet: domain and evidence brief", "excerpt": "The archaeological proposal uses dated charcoal fragments from a site. A fragment date estimates the age of sampled material, not necessarily the date of a building event. The reported interval is 1180 to 1260 CE. The relation between the charcoal and the building remains uncertain.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}, {"id": "S2", "title": "Controlled source packet: experimental design brief", "excerpt": "The team will compare 14 context descriptions. A cautious synthesis must preserve the full date interval and distinguish material age from event timing. Contexts with uncertain association will remain in the report. The study will not select the interval midpoint as an established construction date.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}]}
